@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — the fourth protocol opened against a generalization, and it
# is opened against the head of the queue
#
# SPEC §16 makes the queue for stage three derivable rather than written: the
# generalizations on which no pm:Testing points, ordered by the number of
# attested claims behind them. Run against the holding on 2026-09-17 the head
# of that queue is the claim examined here, with nine predecessors and a tenth
# arriving in the same run. It had never been examined.
#
# The claim divides a field in two and says something about each half. Where
# the judgement of a single performance is fixed to a physical event no party
# can produce at will, the transmission states the failing side as well as the
# succeeding one; where the criterion is instead an appearance announced to the
# participants beforehand, it states the succeeding side alone. Two questions
# therefore had to be put and not one, and they were put separately, which is
# the procedural lesson prima-materia#726 drew from the protocol before this.
#
# Both questions returned something. The first returned a case the claim's
# enumeration does not carry and its own falsifying condition does not catch;
# the second returned a witness that states the failing side where the claim
# says it will not. Neither refutes. Both narrow, and the narrowing is in the
# same direction: what the claim measures is the wording of one witness, and
# the wording moves when the witness changes.
#

pmp:BothSidesOfTheCriterionExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the uncontrollable criterion always carries its failing side"@en, "Ob das unbeherrschbare Kriterium stets seine Misslingensseite trägt"@de ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that a transmission which fixes the judgement of a single performance to a physical event neither party can produce at will states what the event would have to be for the performance to have failed, and that a transmission whose criterion is an appearance announced beforehand states the succeeding side alone. The population is the set of criterion statements in the compiled holding; every node whose wording fixes an outcome of a single performance to a perceptible event was drawn and read at its place, and each was classified on two axes, whether a party to the operation could produce the event at will and whether the text states the other side."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:TheCriterionNoPartyControls ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A transmission that fixes the judgement of a performance to an event no party can produce at will and states the succeeding side alone, with no wording for the case in which the event does not occur. That is the condition the claim names for itself. It was not met: every uncontrollable criterion read in this protocol says something about the other side, and the deviation below is that one of them says something that is not a failing verdict."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Two searches, run as two questions and not as one. The first went over the compiled holding for every node whose wording fixes an outcome to a perceptible event, classified each by controllability, and asked of the uncontrollable ones whether the failing side stands in the text; twenty-two nodes were drawn and read at their places. The second asked the opposite question of the announced-appearance half: which announced criterion states what the absence of the appearance would show. It returned one, from a nineteenth-century corpus, and that is the opposition recorded here. Not searched: anything outside this holding. The claim is about the wording of transmissions and the holding carries some ninety corpora, so the cheap search is the one that was run; a transmission outside it that states an uncontrollable criterion one-sidedly would still refute the claim, and nothing here makes that less likely."@en ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-17" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-18" ;
    skos:note "Eight confirming cases beyond the seven the claim was built on, all found in the first search and all read at their places. The verdict rule of the Lower Congo ordeal names both outcomes and then names four further ways of not clearing oneself, which is more of the failing side than the claim requires. The Maya cane planted before a journey states both readings in advance and instructs the grandmother what to say in each case. The Mithraic report gives the highest initiation to the candidate who is still alive after the ordeals and an honourable burial to the one who has succumbed. The proxy dosing of the accused states what follows when none of the boys shows the signs, and what follows is not an acquittal but a fresh appointment of representatives. The reversed rule for theft and adultery states both sides of the same bark with the sides exchanged. The severed-channel prognosis of the Lingshu pairs each channel with a sign and a day. And the Sámi drum rule of sixteen seventy-four states health for the ring travelling with the sun and sickness for it travelling against."@en ;
    skos:note "The deviation, and it is a third thing the claim's two columns do not have room for. The assay in the fourth chapter of the Baopuzi fixes the judgement of the whole operation to a physical event: two hundred and forty zhu of the elixir joined with a hundred catties of mercury and put to the fire make gold, and where the gold forms the drug is complete. Nobody can make gold form at will, so the criterion is in the first column. The chapter then states the other side and cancels it in the same clause: where the gold does not form, the drug is to be sealed again and fired again for the same count of days, and then there is no case in which it does not form. The failing side is named and abolished, which is neither stating it nor omitting it."@en ;
    skos:note "Why that is a deviation and not a refutation, stated exactly. The claim says that the transmission states what the event would have to be for the performance to have failed; this transmission does state it, in the words where the gold does not form, and then denies that the case exists. A reader of the claim would expect from those words a judgement against the operation and would not get one. The correction the protocol recommends is to split the first column in two: criteria whose failing side is a verdict, and criteria whose failing side is a repetition. This holding already carries the second shape under its own generalization about counted thresholds, and the two statements meet here."@en ;
    skos:note "The opposition to the second clause, and it is the more interesting of the two findings because the claim asserts a silence and a silence is cheap to break. The magnetic literature announces to the operator in advance what he will perceive at the first question, and it names three outcomes and not one: the subject wakes, or he continues to sleep without answering, or he answers without waking. Of the first it says outright that somnambulism has not taken place and is not to be thought of again during that sitting. That is an announced criterion with its failing side stated, which the second clause says does not happen."@en ;
    skos:note "What the same corpus does with that statement two works later is the reason the second clause survives in a narrowed form. It rarely happens, the other work says, that the magnetic sleep is produced at the first sitting, and the first effects are often so little marked that they pass unnoticed, which is no reason to conclude the subject incapable; the operator is to begin again the following day, and the day after, for eight consecutive days, and only then may he form a definitive judgment. The failing side of the first sitting is therefore stated and then made non-binding, exactly as the alchemical assay makes its own non-binding. The two findings of this protocol are the same finding, arrived at from the two halves of the claim."@en ;
    skos:note "The corrected statement, written out here and not carried back into the node, because a claim is corrected where it is examined. Where a transmission fixes the judgement of a single performance to an event no party can produce at will, it states the other side; where the criterion is an appearance announced beforehand, it either states no other side or states one that a further attempt dissolves. What distinguishes the two columns is therefore not whether the other side is stated but whether the transmission lets a single performance settle anything, and in both columns the cases in which it does are the cases in which somebody other than the operator reads the event."@en ;
    skos:note "Three near-misses, recorded because they bound the claim without touching it. The Sámi drum rule is given by two recorders and the later one weakens the negative side, augury of a favourable event with the sun and a doubt arising against it, so that two-sidedness can be a property of a recorder rather than of a transmission. The Shangqing account of the four men who compounded the elixir has two of them die of it and reads their death as the attainment, which is the failing side of an uncontrollable criterion being reinterpreted after the event rather than before it. And the sign of the closed breath in the eighth chapter of the Baopuzi, a goose down that does not stir, was set aside as outside the population: the operator controls his own breath, so the criterion is in neither column."@en ;
    skos:note "What the protocol does not establish, and it is the same limit the claim states about itself. The sample is this holding, the holding was assembled by runs that found stated criteria interesting, and a criterion stated one-sidedly is exactly the kind a compiler passes over. The state is set at cases-with-deviation and not higher for that reason: what has been shown is that within a body of material selected for its criteria, the uncontrollable ones carry their other side, and that at least one of them carries it in a form that decides nothing."@en ;
    skos:note "A second opposition to the second clause was found on 2026-09-18, in a corpus this holding had opened at one piece and not at this work, and it is harder than the first. The demotic manual of London and Leiden carries a direct inquiry ascribed to a named priest in which the two answers are bound to two distinct appearances before anything is seen: if the thing shall happen the god is to come in his form of a priest, in his figure of a servant of the temple; if it shall not come to pass, in his form of a Kalashire. The criterion is an announced appearance, which puts it in the second column; both sides are stated, in consecutive clauses, which the second clause says does not happen there; and no further attempt is prescribed that would dissolve the reading, so the escape the corrected statement above left open is not available here. See pmc:PdmRamshauFormaDuplex and pmp:PdmBothSidesSearch."@en ;
    skos:note "What survives of the second clause after it, and it is less than after the magnetic case. The corrected statement said that an announced criterion either states no other side or states one that a further attempt dissolves; this witness states one and dissolves nothing. What can still be said is narrower and is about who reads the event: the two forms appear in a vision or a dream belonging to one person, that person is the operator himself, and the manual makes no provision for the case in which neither form comes. So the criterion discriminates in its wording and is certified by the only party with an interest in it — which is the property the corrected statement identified as the real divider, arrived at here from the second column instead of the first."@en ;
    skos:note "The same search brought two witnesses for the first clause out of the same manuscript, and they bound the finding rather than adding to it. The pregnancy test of the verso — the woman's water on a herb overnight, scorched for no conception and flourishing for conception — and the assay that tells a genuine stone by whether a little of it ground with water removes the skin are both uncontrollable criteria with both sides stated, and both stand in the medical and pharmacological half of the work. The transmission that cannot register a failed inquiry states a falsifiable prognosis three columns later, and the difference is not the culture and not the century but what kind of thing is being judged."@en ;
    skos:note "A tenth predecessor entered the claim on the day this protocol was written, from the insular Celtic satire whose stated outcome falls on the poets if the poets were in the wrong; it arrives with prima-materia#733 and is not counted among the cases above, because a witness added in the same run as the examination is not a case the examination found."@en .
